---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/chew-magna.json";
---
<Base
  title="Chew Magna"
  eyebrow={`Somerset · found ${d.searched}`}
  dek="The one open question in this archive's English line is where Hannah Saniger was born. A census two streets from her door names a Somerset parish, and it is the first answer that fits every constraint at once.">

  <section class="blk" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="note" style="border-left-width:3px;margin-bottom:0">
      <strong style="font-size:1.1em">This page is wrong, and it is kept here for that reason.</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Everything below was built on the belief that Hannah Saniger was not born in Gloucestershire —
      a belief that came from a single yes/no tick in the 1841 census. The 1851 census, taken in the
      same house, states her birthplace outright: <strong>Berkeley, Gloucestershire</strong>. The
      argument on this page is sound and its premise is false.
      <a href={u("/berkeley-woman/")}>What actually settled it →</a>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every English document this archive holds agrees on one negative: whatever
    <a href={u("/people/hannah-saniger/")}>Hannah Saniger</a> was, she was <strong>not born in
    Gloucestershire</strong>. The <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>1841 census at Drivers Fields</a>
    says so with a tick, and says the same of the seventy-year-old
    <strong>Lyddya Sinegar</strong> living in the house. The
    <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>reconstitution of all 81 Gloucestershire Saniger households</a> says so
    by exhaustion. The <a href={u("/general-hewitt/")}>passenger register</a> dittoed the question
    instead of answering it.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">So the county has to be somewhere else. Here is a Saniger household
    in Bristol ten years later, and it names one.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{d.household.census}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Oxford Road, St Philip and Jacob</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Relation</th><th class="m">Cond.</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Born</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.household.rows.map(([sn, fn, rel, cond, age, occ, cty, pl]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{fn}</strong> <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{sn}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{rel}</td>
              <td class="m small">{cond === "WIDOW" ? <strong>Widow</strong> : cond}</td>
              <td class="m"><strong>{age}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{occ || "—"}</td>
              <td class="small">{cty}, <strong>{pl}</strong></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.7rem">Address as written: <strong>{d.household.address}</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{d.h1841.census}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">And ten years earlier, in the parish itself</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Sex</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Born in county</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.h1841.rows.map(([sn, fn, sx, age, occ, cty]) => (
            <tr style={sn === "SANIGAR" ? "background:var(--tint)" : ""}>
              <td><strong>{fn}</strong> <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{sn}</span></td>
              <td class="m small">{sx}</td><td class="m">{age}</td>
              <td class="small">{occ || "—"}</td><td class="small">{cty}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.7rem">Address as written: <strong>{d.h1841.address}</strong>.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.h1841.reading}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>{d.h1841.means}</strong> <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
      <br /><br />
      {d.h1841.collinson}
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.spellings}
      <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>Lyddya Sinegar →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why it fits</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Four constraints, and a parish that meets all of them</h2>
    <ol class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {d.why.map(([h, b]) => (<li style="margin-bottom:.7rem"><strong>{h}</strong> {b}</li>))}
    </ol>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And why it is not a finding.</strong>
      <ul style="margin:.6rem 0 0 1.1rem">
        {d.against.map((x) => (<li style="margin-bottom:.35rem">{x}</li>))}
      </ul>
      <div style="margin-top:.8rem">This is a <em>place to look</em>, arrived at by elimination and
      proximity. It is the best one this archive has had, and it is still a hypothesis.
      <span class="chip inf">a lead, not a link</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Collinson's Somerset, 1791</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">What the place was</h2>

    <p class="lede">{d.collinson.desc}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And one line changes how this lead reads.</strong> {d.collinson.cloth}
      <br /><br />
      Every Saniger this archive has found in Gloucestershire is in cloth — a clothier at Saniger in
      1692, weavers, tuckers and curriers at Cam and Dursley through the eighteenth century, and a
      family that leaves for Bristol when the trade collapses. Chew Magna is a decayed clothing town
      six miles from Bristol on the other side of the county line.
      <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>The trade, across the whole surname →</a>
      <span class="chip inf">a pattern, not a proof</span>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>A records fact worth having before anyone writes to Taunton.</strong>
      {d.collinson.peculiar} <a href={u("/errands/")}>On the queue →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.collinson.null}
      <span class="chip doc">read, and empty</span></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Two numbers on this page were wrong when it was published this morning.</strong>
      {d.correction}
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Where those go →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Coverage before absence</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The obvious next search cannot be run</h2>

    <p class="lede">{d.found}</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">That looks like a null result. It is not, and the rule on this site
    is to find out which before saying anything. {d.coverage.test}</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th class="m">Somerset entries</th><th class="m">Parishes</th><th class="m">At Chew Magna</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.coverage.rows.map(([who, n, par, cm]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{who}</strong></td><td class="m">{n}</td><td class="m">{par}</td><td class="m"><strong>{cm}</strong></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">{d.coverage.verdict}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>{d.coverage.so}</strong>
      <span class="chip doc">coverage established by control test</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What happens next</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Chew Magna's own register, 1795–1815.</strong> The
      originals are at the Somerset Heritage Centre and filmed on the subscription sites. A baptism
      for Hannah Saniger, daughter of John and Lydia, would close the oldest open question on this
      site in one line. <a href={u("/errands/")}>On the queue →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>And it would test the tree at the same time.</strong>
      The family tree says Hannah was born at <em>Berkeley</em> on 23 July 1805, daughter of John
      Saniger who died there in 1824. The 1841 census says she was not born in Gloucestershire, and
      Berkeley is in Gloucestershire. Those two statements cannot both be true.
      <a href={u("/berkeley/")}>What Berkeley holds →</a></li>
      <li><strong>The Bristol chapels remain the other half of the answer.</strong> Twelve RG 4
      volumes can hold a birth in this window. <a href={u("/bristol-chapels/")}>They are listed →</a></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>
